# Porto Features - Cursor Rules

## Project Purpose

Shared BDD contracts (Gherkin) and fixtures aligned with [porto-data](https://github.com/gruncellka/porto-data). Step definitions and execution live outside this repo.

## Structure

```
porto_features/                     # Published package content
├── contracts/                      # Machine-readable contracts
├── matrix/                         # Coverage index (sdk, canary, orders)
├── features/
│   ├── sdk/
│   │   ├── core/                   # @sdk @core
│   │   └── providers/{operator}/   # @sdk @operator:{id}
│   └── adapters/{operator}/        # @adapters @operator:{id} @wire:{adapter}
└── fixtures/                       # Test data
    └── addresses/
```

## Gherkin Format

Every Feature declares exactly one of `@sdk` or `@adapters`. See `docs/vocabulary.md` for canonical steps.

## Key Principles

- **Implementation-agnostic**: Features describe behavior, not runner internals
- **Data-driven**: Use fixtures for test data
- **BDD**: Business-readable scenarios
- **Package-first**: Keep data under `porto_features/` so npm and PyPI ship identical files

## Generated adapter matrix

`orders.generated.yaml` and adapter `mark_order` Examples are produced from porto-data wire by Porto SDK Lab `matrix-orders-sync.py` — do not hand-edit.

## Do NOT

- Add step definitions here — those belong in implementor test suites
- Reference runner internals in feature files
- Duplicate fixtures that exist in porto-data
